WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.538
You were building a routine. Then you missed a day. Maybe two.

2
00:00:07.538 --> 00:00:12.615
Suddenly, your mind says, ‘I’ve ruined it. There’s no point continuing.’

3
00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:19.025
A setback can feel discouraging, especially when the goal matters to you. But

4
00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:24.093
one attempt gives you information about that attempt. It doesn’t describe

5
00:00:24.093 --> 00:00:25.935
everything you’re capable of.

6
00:00:25.935 --> 00:00:31.533
Imagine planning a daily walk and missing several days during a demanding

7
00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:31.999
week.

8
00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:36.599
Before deciding you failed, ask: What made this difficult?

9
00:00:36.599 --> 00:00:41.663
Was the timing unrealistic? Were you exhausted? Did something unexpected need

10
00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:42.583
your attention?

11
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:46.741
Then ask: What could make another attempt more manageable?

12
00:00:46.741 --> 00:00:53.209
You might choose a shorter walk, a different time, or someone to join you.

13
00:00:53.629 --> 00:00:59.269
Sometimes the useful adjustment is rest. Sometimes the goal itself needs to

14
00:00:59.269 --> 00:00:59.739
change.

15
00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:06.752
You don’t need to make up for a missed day by demanding twice as much

16
00:01:06.752 --> 00:01:07.219
tomorrow.

17
00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:13.527
Think about one plan that hasn’t gone as expected. What would a fair restart

18
00:01:13.527 --> 00:01:14.428
look like?

19
00:01:14.848 --> 00:01:20.852
Your earlier efforts still happened. You can learn from this moment and decide

20
00:01:20.852 --> 00:01:22.238
what comes next.
